(1) the theorem Ax(0+x=x) as a datum, under the track's L1 code (theories with 9 axioms: Q1-Q7, Dlt, one form)
  T_Ind (track derivation)     lines  11  bits   220.6  independent check: ok
  T_CVI (direct derivation)    lines  37  bits   872.8  independent check: ok
  extra cost of the theorem in T_CVI: 652.2 bits (the track's per-use overhead of Ind in T_CVI: 880.4)

(2) derive or memorise?  cost of an induction theorem theta as a datum:
    derive in Q+Ind      = bits of the derivation (8 axioms, as in c1_costs.py section (c))
    memorise theta       = prior LAM*|theta| once + a one-line citation log2(10)+log2(9) per occurrence
  Ax 0+x=x                     derive  220.1   memorise: prior  31.7 + cite  6.5   memorising cheaper at first occurrence: yes; derivation wins only if the axiom prior exceeds 30.5 bits/symbol  [ok]
  Ax Ay Sx+y=S(x+y)            derive  551.4   memorise: prior  58.8 + cite  6.5   memorising cheaper at first occurrence: yes; derivation wins only if the axiom prior exceeds 41.9 bits/symbol  [ok]
  Ax x+0=x  (in T_left)        derive  220.1   memorise: prior  31.7 + cite  6.5   memorising cheaper at first occurrence: yes; derivation wins only if the axiom prior exceeds 30.5 bits/symbol  [ok]
  Ax Ay x+Sy=S(x+y) (T_left)   derive  599.8   memorise: prior  58.8 + cite  6.5   memorising cheaper at first occurrence: yes; derivation wins only if the axiom prior exceeds 45.6 bits/symbol  [ok]
Reading: under the track's own code a theorem that needs one induction costs 6-9 times more to derive than to
state.  So on data consisting of distinct induction theorems given without proof, "Q + the theorems as axioms"
beats Q+Ind on every datum, and Q+Ind is preferred only if the derivations are much shorter than in this
library or the axiom prior is much steeper (30-46 bits per symbol here, against 4.5) -- or if the data are drawn from
Q+Ind's own derivation process (well-specified case).
